.page ping 1 send ICMP echo requests to a host

# SYNOPSIS
*ping* [*-c* _COUNT_] [*-i* _SECONDS_] [*-W* _SECONDS_] [*-s* _BYTES_] [*-t* _TTL_] [*-q*] _HOST_

# DESCRIPTION
`ping` sends an ICMP echo request to _HOST_ (a name or a dotted address) once a second and prints a line for every answer: the size of the reply, the sender, the sequence number, the time to live that was left in it, and the round-trip time. Without `-c` it goes on until interrupted with Ctrl+C. At the end it prints how many packets were sent and received and the shortest, average and longest round trip.

It uses an ICMP datagram socket, so any user may run it. Times are measured with the one-millisecond clock of the kernel: a reply on the same machine shows as 0 or 1 ms.

If a router or the stack itself answers with an error, the line says so (`Destination Host Unreachable`, `Time to live exceeded`) instead of a reply. A request that gets no answer within the timeout prints `Request timeout for icmp_seq N`.

# OPTIONS
@ *-c* _COUNT_
    Stop after COUNT requests.
@ *-i* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long between requests (at least 0.2, default 1).
@ *-W* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long for each answer (default 2).
@ *-s* _BYTES_
    Size of the data in the request (8 to 1400, default 56).
@ *-t* _TTL_
    Time to live of the outgoing packets.
@ *-q*
    Quiet: only the summary.

# EXAMPLES
    ping -c 3 10.0.2.2
    ping -c 1 localhost

# EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one answer arrived, 1 if none did, 2 for a usage error or a name that does not resolve.

# SEE ALSO
nslookup(1), ifconfig(1), network(7)
